				<!-- start content -->
				<div id=3D"mw-content-text" class=3D"mw-body-content"><div class=3D"mw-=
content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang=3D"en" dir=3D"ltr"><p>The <a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPU" title=3D"PPU">PPU</a> exposes eight memory-ma=
pped registers to the CPU. These nominally sit at $2000 through $2007 in th=
e CPU's address space, but because their addresses are incompletely decoded=
, they're <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/Mirroring#Memory_Mirroring=
" title=3D"Mirroring">mirrored</a> in every 8 bytes from $2008 through $3FF=
F. For example, a write to $3456 is the same as a write to $2006.
</p><p>The PPU starts rendering immediately after power-on or reset, but ig=
nores writes to most registers (specifically $2000, $2001, $2005 and $2006)=
 until reaching the pre-render scanline of the next frame; more specificall=
y, for around 29658 NTSC CPU cycles or 33132 PAL CPU cycles, assuming the C=
PU and PPU are reset at the same time. See <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.or=
g/wiki/PPU_power_up_state" title=3D"PPU power up state">PPU power up state<=
/a> and <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/Init_code" title=3D"Init cod=
e">Init code</a> for details.
</p>
